Title

Hamiltonian‑Astrophysical Genetics Simulation Engine (H‑AGSE):A Unified Energy‑Landscape Architecture for Multi‑Domain Scientific Simulation

Abstract

The Hamiltonian‑Astrophysical Genetics Simulation Engine (H‑AGSE) is a unified computational architecture designed to model astrophysical structures, genetic regulatory networks, and molecular dynamics using a single Hamiltonian energy formulation. The system integrates multi‑scale datasets, applies high‑performance computational methods, and enforces strict encryption standards to ensure secure scientific processing. H‑AGSE demonstrates that energy‑minimization principles can be applied consistently across 33 orders of magnitude of physical scale, establishing a new class of cross‑domain simulation frameworks.

1. Introduction

Scientific modeling is traditionally fragmented across domains. Astrophysics simulations, molecular dynamics engines, and genetic regulatory models operate independently, despite sharing underlying mathematical principles. H‑AGSE addresses this fragmentation by establishing a unified Hamiltonian framework capable of representing physical, biological, and molecular phenomena through consistent energy‑minimization rules.

This document provides a formal description of the mathematical foundation, system architecture, cross‑scale mapping, validation pathways, and implications of the H‑AGSE framework.

2. Mathematical Foundation

2.1 Core Hamiltonian Formulations

The system is governed by two Hamiltonian expressions:

[ H = -J \sum \sigma_i \sigma_j - h \sum \sigma_i ]

[ H = -J \sum \alpha_i \sigma_i - h \sum \sigma_i ]

2.2 Variable Definitions

Spin variables (σᵢ): Discrete state units representing density nodes (astrophysics), gene regulatory states (genetics), or microstate conformations (molecular biology).

Coupling constant (J): Interaction strength between neighboring states.

External field (h): Domain‑wide influence acting on all states.

Weighting term (αᵢ): Domain‑specific modifiers such as dark‑matter density, transcription factor strength, or local potential energy.

3. System Architecture

3.1 Input Layer

The engine accepts astrophysical datasets, genetic and molecular datasets, and Hamiltonian blueprint parameters.

3.2 Encryption Layer

All data entering the system is encrypted using AES‑256, FIPS 140‑2 compliant modules, and HSM‑based key management.

3.3 H‑Core Engine

The core engine includes a Hamiltonian solver, Ising model mapping, perturbation analysis, and multi‑domain state translation.

3.4 Computation Layer

Cloud and HPC infrastructure execute simulation modules for gene regulatory networks, protein folding, cellular dynamics, and cosmic‑web formation.

3.5 Visualization Layer

A 3D rendering pipeline provides visual representations of energy landscapes, folding pathways, regulatory stability, and astrophysical structure formation.

3.6 Security Layer

Role‑based access control, audit logging, TLS 1.3, and double‑encryption pathways ensure secure operation.

4. Cross‑Scale Mapping (33 Orders of Magnitude)

H‑AGSE applies the same Hamiltonian formulation across physical scales ranging from molecular to cosmic.

4.1 Molecular Scale (~10⁻⁹ m)

Protein folding and microstate stabilization.

4.2 Genetic/Cellular Scale (~10⁻⁶–10⁻⁵ m)

Regulatory network energy landscapes and stable expression patterns.

4.3 Mesoscopic Scale (~10⁻³–10⁻¹ m)

Collective cellular behavior and emergent tissue‑level configurations.

4.4 Cosmic Scale (~10²⁴ m)

Dark‑matter lattice formation and cosmic‑web coherence.

Unified Principle

Stable configurations occur at minima of the Hamiltonian (H), regardless of domain.

5. Validation Pathways

Validation includes cross‑domain benchmarking, perturbation response analysis, stability and convergence testing, multi‑scale consistency verification, and comparative evaluation against domain‑specific engines.

6. Implications and Future Work

H‑AGSE establishes a new class of unified simulation architecture. Future developments include integration with quantum‑scale Hamiltonian models, real‑time astrophysical dataset ingestion, expanded biological modeling, and collaborative research across physics, biology, and computational science.

